I did so because define:web is liable to return things unrelated to the
world wide web, while define:www is unlikely to do so.  Indeed, looking
at define:web it seems to be about half unrelated definitions and many
of the related ones are rather useless ("Short for World Wide Web." or
variants thereof).

At any rate, you are the one who is cherry picking definitions.  You
can go down the entire list from both queries and see that while many
of them are HTML or hypertext specific, only a few explicitly admit
non-HTML documents (many others are silent on the matter, saying things
like "World Wide Web, a part of the Internet.", or "a highly graphical
computer-based information network.").

Going to more authoritative sources like FOLDOC and Tim Berners-Lee's
FAQ confirms that more technical minds don't consider outside systems
that happen to have support in modern browsers to be part of the WWW.

You could certainly argue that "everything that can be linked" or
"everything that has a URI" or whatever is an acceptable alternate
definition, but "this is Usenet, not a stinking web forum" certainly
uses an acceptable definition (and what appears to me to be the
predominant one) correctly.

